    "marks": 10,
    "model": "Periodisation is dividing a training plan into planned phases or cycles, each with its own aim, so that volume, intensity and recovery vary systematically to bring the athlete to peak performance at a target competition. The macrocycle covers the year or season and is divided into mesocycles of several weeks with one focus, which are made up of weekly microcycles. The year moves through a preparation phase (general base, then specific fitness), a competition phase (maintenance and peaking) and a transition phase (active rest). It is important because an athlete cannot hold peak form all year: it lets the coach build qualities in a logical order, plan recovery to avoid overtraining and injury, and taper before the championship so the athlete arrives fit and fresh. For a championship held every four years, each year's plan builds towards that target.",
    "prompt": "Explain periodisation and outline its importance in preparing for a major championship.",
    "type": "short"
