   {
    "marks": 12,
    "model": "Activity: basketball. The initial analysis used a technique checklist and video, which showed the elbow flaring out and the release point varying; a success rate out of a set number of attempts, which fell under pressure; a fitness test battery, which showed endurance fading late in games; and notational counts, which showed the player shooting when a team-mate was better placed. The four areas for development are therefore technical (the shooting action), fitness (endurance), tactical (shot selection) and psychological (concentration under pressure). Each becomes a performance goal: a process goal to keep the elbow under the ball, a performance goal to raise success under pressure, a fitness goal to hold shooting accuracy late in games, and a tactical goal to pass when a team-mate is better placed. The plan matches methods to goals: fixed practice for the shooting action, a conditioned endurance routine for fitness, pressure drills for concentration, and variable practice with defenders for shot selection, with the load progressed week by week. Progress is monitored with a training log, video recorded at intervals and checked against the same checklist, and a mid-programme retest; the plan is adapted when a drill stops improving its goal. At the end the same methods are repeated under the same conditions: the checklist, the success rate, the fitness battery and the notational counts of shot selection. Each goal is judged achieved, partly achieved or not achieved from the before-and-after results, so the effectiveness of the programme is shown.",
    "prompt": "Explain how information from a performance analysis can be used to plan for optimum performance, and how the effectiveness of the plan can be evidenced.",
    "type": "short"
   }
